---
import Layout from '../layouts/Layout.astro';
import WorkflowFlow from '../components/WorkflowFlow.vue';
import { home } from '../content/home';
---

<Layout title={home.title} description={home.description}>
	<link rel="preconnect" href="https://fonts.googleapis.com" slot="head" />
	<link rel="preconnect" href="https://fonts.gstatic.com" crossorigin slot="head" />
	<link
		href="https://fonts.googleapis.com/css2?family=IBM+Plex+Mono:wght@400;500&family=Manrope:wght@400;500;600;700;800&family=Sora:wght@600;700;800&display=swap"
		rel="stylesheet"
		slot="head"
	/>
	<div class="page">
		<header class="topbar">
			<a class="brand" href="#top" aria-label="ConfigButler home">
				<span class="brand__logo-frame">
					<img class="brand__logo" src="/logo_owl.png" alt="ConfigButler owl logo" />
				</span>
				<span class="brand__text">
					<strong>ConfigButler</strong>
					<span>{home.tagline}</span>
				</span>
			</a>
			<nav class="topnav" aria-label="Primary">
				{home.nav.map((item) => (
					<a href={item.href}>{item.label}</a>
				))}
			</nav>
			<a class="button button--ghost topbar__cta" href={home.primaryCta.href}>{home.primaryCta.label}</a>
		</header>

		<section class="hero" id="top">
			<div class="hero__copy">
				<div class="hero__brand-lockup">
					<span class="hero__logo-frame">
						<img class="hero__logo" src="/logo_owl.png" alt="ConfigButler owl logo" />
					</span>
					<div class="hero__brand-meta">
						<p class="hero__brand-name">ConfigButler</p>
						<p class="hero__brand-tagline">{home.tagline}</p>
					</div>
				</div>
				<div class="hero__badge">{home.tagline}</div>
				<p class="hero__eyebrow">API-first. Agent-ready. Git-recorded.</p>
				<h1>{home.headline}</h1>
				<p class="hero__lede">{home.subheadline}</p>
				<div class="hero__actions">
					<a class="button button--primary" href={home.primaryCta.href}>{home.primaryCta.label}</a>
					<a class="button button--secondary" href={home.secondaryCta.href}>{home.secondaryCta.label}</a>
				</div>
				<p class="hero__supporting">
					ConfigButler does not replace GitOps. It gives GitOps a better front door.
				</p>
			</div>
		</section>

		<section class="hero-flow">
			<WorkflowFlow client:load examples={home.intentExamples} />
		</section>

		<section class="page-section" id="problem">
			<div class="section-heading">
				<p class="section-kicker">Problem</p>
				<h2>Configuration is still stuck in the wrong places</h2>
				<p class="section-lede">
					Important configuration is spread across Git repositories, SaaS admin screens, YAML files, dashboards, scripts, and ticket-driven workflows.
				</p>
			</div>
			<div class="problem-grid">
				<div class="problem-grid__copy">
					<p>That creates a painful choice:</p>
					<ul class="bullet-list">
						{home.problemChoices.map((item) => (
							<li>{item}</li>
						))}
					</ul>
				</div>
				<div class="problem-grid__panel">
					<p class="quote-card">
						ConfigButler provides a safer control layer for configuration.
					</p>
				</div>
			</div>
		</section>

		<section class="page-section" id="solution">
			<div class="section-heading">
				<p class="section-kicker">Solution</p>
				<h2>One controlled front door for configuration</h2>
				<p class="section-lede">
					ConfigButler exposes configuration as typed intent.
				</p>
			</div>
			<p class="section-body">
				Humans can use a clean GUI. Automation can use an API. AI agents get a safe interface designed for action, not guesswork.
			</p>
			<p class="section-body">
				Behind the scenes, ConfigButler validates the request, applies policy, and records the accepted change as a Git commit or pull request.
			</p>
			<div class="card-grid">
				{home.solutionCards.map((card) => (
					<article class="info-card">
						<h3>{card.title}</h3>
						<p>{card.copy}</p>
					</article>
				))}
			</div>
		</section>

		<section class="page-section page-section--accent" id="gitops">
			<div class="section-heading">
				<p class="section-kicker">First use case</p>
				<h2>Start with your GitOps repo</h2>
				<p class="section-lede">
					Have a GitOps repository where routine configuration changes still require YAML edits or manual pull requests?
				</p>
			</div>
			<div class="two-column">
				<div>
					<p class="section-body">
						ConfigButler can turn one workflow into a self-service API and GUI, while keeping Git as the audit trail.
					</p>
					<ul class="bullet-list bullet-list--compact">
						{home.workflows.map((workflow) => (
							<li>{workflow}</li>
						))}
					</ul>
				</div>
				<aside class="note-card">
					<p>ConfigButler does not replace Flux, Argo CD, or your existing GitOps pipeline.</p>
					<p>It gives GitOps a better front door.</p>
				</aside>
			</div>
		</section>

		<section class="page-section" id="how-it-works">
			<div class="section-heading">
				<p class="section-kicker">How it works</p>
				<h2>How it works</h2>
				<p class="section-lede">
					Git remains useful for history, review, ownership, promotion, and reconciliation. It just no longer has to be the only interface.
				</p>
			</div>
			<div class="steps-grid">
				{home.steps.map((step, index) => (
					<article class="step-card">
						<div class="step-card__number">0{index + 1}</div>
						<h3>{step.title}</h3>
						<p>{step.copy}</p>
					</article>
				))}
			</div>
		</section>

		<section class="page-section" id="agents">
			<div class="section-heading">
				<p class="section-kicker">Agents</p>
				<h2>Built for humans. Ready for agents.</h2>
				<p class="section-lede">
					AI agents should work on the outcome, not reverse-engineer configuration screens.
				</p>
			</div>
			<div class="two-column">
				<div>
					<p class="section-body">
						Most configuration systems were designed for human operators clicking through admin panels or engineers editing files by hand. That makes them awkward and risky for agents.
					</p>
					<p class="section-body">
						ConfigButler gives agents a safer interface:
					</p>
					<ul class="bullet-list bullet-list--compact">
						{home.agentPoints.map((item) => (
							<li>{item}</li>
						))}
					</ul>
				</div>
				<div class="quote-card quote-card--tall">
					<p>Let your agents solve the problem. Let ConfigButler handle the configuration workflow.</p>
					<p class="quote-card__subtext">Built for platform teams today. Ready for AI agents tomorrow.</p>
				</div>
			</div>
		</section>

		<section class="page-section page-section--accent" id="pilot">
			<div class="section-heading">
				<p class="section-kicker">Pilot offer</p>
				<h2>Turn one painful configuration workflow into a working pilot</h2>
				<p class="section-lede">
					The first commercial offer is intentionally focused.
				</p>
			</div>
			<div class="two-column">
				<div>
					<p class="section-body">
						In a short pilot, we take one existing configuration workflow and turn it into a working self-service API and GUI with Git-backed change recording.
					</p>
					<ul class="bullet-list bullet-list--compact">
						{home.pilotItems.map((item) => (
							<li>{item}</li>
						))}
					</ul>
				</div>
				<div class="cta-card">
					<p class="cta-card__kicker">Configuration workflow pilot</p>
					<p class="cta-card__copy">
						Start with one high-friction workflow. Prove the operating model. Keep the Git-based controls you already trust.
					</p>
					<a class="button button--primary button--full" href={home.primaryCta.href}>{home.primaryCta.label}</a>
					<p class="cta-card__footnote">Focused scope. Clear handover. Practical next steps.</p>
				</div>
			</div>
		</section>

		<section class="page-section" id="credibility">
			<div class="section-heading">
				<p class="section-kicker">Credibility</p>
				<h2>Built from real GitOps experiments</h2>
				<p class="section-lede">
					ConfigButler is informed by practical work around GitOps, Kubernetes APIs, platform engineering, and configuration ownership.
				</p>
			</div>
			<ul class="bullet-list bullet-list--compact">
				{home.credibilityItems.map((item) => (
					<li>{item}</li>
				))}
			</ul>
		</section>

		<section class="final-cta">
			<div class="final-cta__content">
				<p class="section-kicker">Final CTA</p>
				<h2>Have a configuration workflow that should not require YAML edits?</h2>
				<p>
					Let's turn one real workflow into a safer API, GUI, and Git-backed change trail.
				</p>
			</div>
			<a class="button button--primary" href={home.primaryCta.href}>{home.primaryCta.label}</a>
		</section>

		<footer class="site-footer">
			<div>
				<p class="site-footer__brand">ConfigButler</p>
				<p class="site-footer__tagline">{home.tagline}</p>
			</div>
			<nav class="site-footer__links" aria-label="Footer">
				{home.footerLinks.map((link) => (
					<a href={link.href}>{link.label}</a>
				))}
			</nav>
			<p class="site-footer__copyright">Copyright 2026 ConfigButler</p>
		</footer>
	</div>
</Layout>
